Z||000005||MR2KLR||12-16-2001||09:31 PM||||I have the pictured rims on my 76 Datsun 260Z I payed $1,300 W/ 205/40 Nitto 555's on them. About a week later I saw them for the first time in Sport Compact car selling for $1,000 for 17X7's W/ 205/40 Tires. That was this past April, So they may have come down more. I'll have to get a pic scaned even Though the car looks rough (broken Lights & lots of rust) it's wheels are prety. But be warned they are ALOT darker in person. The farther away you getthe darker the wheels look. From 100 yards away they are black.<P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||California Kid||12-16-2001||09:51 PM||||Thanks for the responses so far. A picture of these on a Fiero would be worth a thousand words (17's on front, 18's on rear).<P>Realize they are not the real deal like multi-piece racing wheels, but they look real nice. Hard for me to justify $2000 for the real thing.<P>Just a touch of the winter blaws, thinking about next season, and what would improve the appearance of the car. I like the OZ's on it now, just looking for that extra edge.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Wheel_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||12-16-2001||10:51 PM||||Hi Tom: real quick and dirty in photoshop   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/koeing-wheels.jpg">  <P>I even added some upgraded brakes!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/koeing-wheels-brakes.jpg">  <P>I like your OZs too, give the car a very 'muscular' appearance, but to be honest I've been seceretly wondering for a while when you were going to setp up to 17+s   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">     <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||California Kid||12-16-2001||11:07 PM||||Hey Ben....Thanks very much for the pictures!!! Appreciate you taking the time to do this.<P>The main reason I stayed away from 17 inch wheels be because of the Michigan roads. We probably have the worst chuck holes in the country, and they eat tires and wheels three times a day!!! The other reason I'm a little reluctant to go that direction is that the car sticks like glue now at 1.17g.  On my road surfaces I know the 17 and 18's are going to produce a real slap in the ass ride, just part of the price you pay for going to the top.<P>Pretty tough decision as I'm not really looking for performance improvement.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tina||12-16-2001||11:08 PM||||Nice wheels,<BR>but how many toothbrushes would it take to get these puppies clean.<P>Tina<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||12-16-2001||11:34 PM||||Ooooo...<P>You may have to be careful with the 18s in the rear, especially as they are not fordged, they may bend easially...<P>The more expensive 2 or 3-piece forged wheels are much stronger, and won't bend/dent as easially.  You pay for it though. (my BBS/porsche 18" wheels&tyres cost me about $700/corner USED.)<P>Actually, I didn't notice any signifigant decrease in ride quality going from STOCK 15"s to my 17"s, infact the ride actually seened to get smoother. (yes, it's very weird...) and the rubber 'slop' of the tyres is gone. The car just *goes* now.  I still have 3.8" of sidwall on the 17s, with 215/45/17 which is more than enough for rural CA roads so far.<P>Yeah, tough call for you though. 1.17g is pretty darn good! Our little cars are pretty amazing <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Are the MI roads really *that* bad?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Hrm, maybe keep the 16s for 'around town' driving wheels, and use the 17/18s as your roadrace/show wheels?  Or just toss them on, with some fairly big rubber, and see what happens :S)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: Cleaning wise, they're probally big enough to get a rag in there between the spokes.  Also, if you have a carbon-type compound pad, the dust will just give them that darker 'freshly raced' dirty look  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>Hey Ben....Thanks very much for the pictures!!! Appreciate you taking the time to do this.<P>The main reason I stayed away from 17 inch wheels be because of the Michigan roads. We probably have the worst chuck holes in the country, and they eat tires and wheels three times a day!!! The other reason I'm a little reluctant to go that direction is that the car sticks like glue now at 1.17g.  On my road surfaces I know the 17 and 18's are going to produce a real slap in the ass ride, just part of the price you pay for going to the top.<P>Pretty tough decision as I'm not really looking for performance improvement.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
